{"body":"## Business Context & Purpose\n\nThe `rad-artifact` CLI can record where artifacts live (via `locate`) but cannot actually fetch them. Users must manually copy URLs and use external tools (curl, wget) to download artifacts. Adding a `download` command closes this gap — users can retrieve artifacts by CID from registered locations, with content integrity automatically verified against the CID's hash.\n\nThe design uses a `Fetcher` trait to dispatch by URL scheme, making it straightforward to add new protocols (IPFS, Iroh, etc.) later without modifying existing code.\n\n## Expected Behavior/Outcome\n\n```\nrad-artifact download <oid> <cid> [-o output-path] [--url <url>]\n```\n\n- Looks up the release by OID, finds the artifact by CID\n- Collects URLs from registered locations (or uses `--url` override)\n- Tries each URL in order until one succeeds\n- Verifies downloaded content against the CID (sha2-256 hash)\n- Writes to output path (defaults to artifact name in cwd)\n- Warns on stderr if the artifact is redacted\n- Read-only command (no signer, no sync needed)\n\n## Design Decisions\n\n- **Sync, not async.** The entire codebase is synchronous. Use `ureq` (sync HTTP client) instead of `reqwest`/tokio to avoid pulling in the async ecosystem.\n- **Trait-based dispatch.** A `Fetcher` trait with a `scheme()` method lets a registry pick the right handler per URL. Simple `Vec<Box<dyn Fetcher>>` — no enum dispatch, no generics gymnastics.\n- **CID verification.** After download, compute sha2-256 of the content, construct a CID, and compare. This is the point of content-addressing.\n- **Try URLs in order.** Use `all_locations()` to get every registered URL, try each until one succeeds. Allow `--url` override for direct fetching.\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n### 1. New file: `src/download.rs`\n\nCore download module in the library:\n\n- `Fetcher` trait with `scheme()` and `fetch()` methods\n- `HttpsFetcher` implementing `Fetcher` for `https`/`http` schemes using `ureq`\n- `verify_cid(data, expected_cid)` — computes sha2-256, wraps in multihash, constructs CIDv1 with raw codec (0x55), compares\n- `download(urls, expected_cid, dest, fetchers)` — main logic: tries URLs in order, verifies CID, writes to dest\n- `default_fetchers()` — returns `[HttpsFetcher]`\n- `FetchError` enum (thiserror): `UnsupportedScheme`, `Http`, `Io`, `CidMismatch`, `NoLocations`, `AllFailed`\n\n### 2. Modified: `src/lib.rs`\n\nAdd `pub mod download;` alongside existing `display` and `error` modules.\n\n### 3. Modified: `Cargo.toml`\n\nAdd dependencies:\n- `ureq = \"3\"` — sync HTTP client\n- `sha2 = \"0.10\"` — for CID hash verification\n\n### 4. Modified: `src/bin/rad-artifact.rs`\n\nAdd `Download` command struct with clap derive, handler function, and error types. Follows the read-only pattern of `Show`/`List`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rad-artifact download <oid> <cid>` fetches artifact from registered HTTPS locations\n- [ ] Downloaded content is verified against the CID hash before writing to disk\n- [ ] `--output` flag allows specifying custom output path\n- [ ] `--url` flag allows overriding registered locations with a direct URL\n- [ ] Warning printed to stderr when downloading a redacted artifact\n- [ ] `FetchError::CidMismatch` returned when content doesn't match expected CID\n- [ ] `Fetcher` trait is extensible — new protocols can be added without modifying existing code\n- [ ] `cargo build`, `cargo test`, `cargo clippy` all pass\n- [ ] Existing tests unaffected\n\n## Dependencies & Constraints\n\n- New crate dependencies: `ureq` (sync HTTP), `sha2` (hash verification)\n- CIDs use sha2-256 (code 0x12) with raw codec (0x55) — verification must match this\n- `all_locations()` returns `Vec<&Url>` — URLs from all contributing DIDs\n\n## Files to Modify\n\n- `src/download.rs` — **new** — Fetcher trait, HttpsFetcher, CID verification, download logic\n- `src/lib.rs` — add `pub mod download;`\n- `Cargo.toml` — add `ureq` and `sha2`\n- `src/bin/rad-artifact.rs` — add Download command + handler + errors","type":"comment"}